500 \"S&P 500\") component |\n| Industry | [Payment processing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Payment_processing \"Payment processing\") |\n| Founded | 2000;26 years ago(2000), as a [National Data Corporation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Data_Corporation \"National Data Corporation\") subsidiary 2001;25 years ago(2001), spun off from parent company |\n| Headquarters | Three [Alliance Center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alliance_Center \"Alliance Center\") [Atlanta](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlanta \"Atlanta\"), Georgia, U.S. |\n| Key people | Cameron M. Bready ([president](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_(corporate_title) \"President (corporate title)\") and [CEO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_executive_officer \"Chief executive officer\")) Andréa Carter ([CHRO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_human_resources_officer \"Chief human resources officer\")) David Rumph ([CFO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_financial_officer \"Chief financial officer\")) Ryan Loy ([CTO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_technology_officer \"Chief technology officer\")) |\n| Revenue | ![Image 5: Increase](https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Increase2.svg/20px-Increase2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)[US$](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_dollar \"United States dollar\")10.1 billion (2024) |\n| [Operating income](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earnings_before_interest_and_taxes \"Earnings before interest and taxes\") | ![Image 6: Increase](https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Increase2.svg/20px-Increase2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)US$2.33 billion (2024) |\n| [Net income](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_income \"Net income\") | ![Image 7: Increase](https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Increase2.svg/20px-Increase2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)US$1.57 billion (2024) |\n| [Total assets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asset \"Asset\") | ![Image 8: Decrease](https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Decrease2.svg/20px-Decrease2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)US$46.9 billion (2024) |\n| [Total equity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equity_(finance) \"Equity (finance)\") | ![Image 9: Decrease](https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Decrease2.svg/20px-Decrease2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)US$22.3 billion (2024) |\n| Number of employees | 27,000 (2024) |\n| [Subsidiaries](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subsidiary \"Subsidiary\") | * [Heartland Payment Systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heartland_Payment_Systems \"Heartland Payment Systems\") * [Worldpay, Inc.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worldpay,_Inc. \"Worldpay, Inc.\") |\n| Website | [globalpayments.com](http://globalpayments.com/) |\n| **Footnotes** [[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-10-K_2024-1) |\n\n**Global Payments Inc.** is an American multinational [financial technology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_technology \"Financial technology\") company that provides [payment](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_transaction \"Financial transaction\") technology to merchants, issuers and consumers.[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-2) In June 2021, the company was named to the [Fortune 500](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortune_500 \"Fortune 500\").[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-3) The company processes payments made through [credit cards](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Credit_card \"Credit card\"), [debit cards](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debit_card \"Debit card\"),[[4]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-tsysmerger-4) and digital and [contactless payments](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contactless_payment \"Contactless payment\").[[5]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-glimmers-5)\n\n## History\n\n[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Global_Payments&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: History\")]\n\nGlobal Payments was founded in 1996 and spun off from [National Data Corporation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Data_Corporation \"National Data Corporation\"), its former parent company, in 2001.[[6]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-6) Global Payments has been an independent, publicly traded company on the [New York Stock Exchange](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Stock_Exchange \"New York Stock Exchange\") having the ticker symbol “GPN” since its spin off.\n\nIn 2009, it paid $75 million for United Card Service, [Russia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russia \"Russia\")'s leading credit card processing company.[[7]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-Filkins2018.10.15-7) In 2011, Global Payments's United Card Service bought [Alfa-Bank](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfa-Bank \"Alfa-Bank\")'s credit card processing unit.[[7]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-Filkins2018.10.15-7) In October 2012, it acquired the smaller Accelerated Payment Technologies for $413 million.[[8]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-8) In October 2014, it purchased Australian payment processing company Ezidebit for $305 million.[[9]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-9) In January 2015, it bought Payment Processing (also known as PayPros), a [California](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California \"California\") company, for $420 million.[[10]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-10)\n\nIn March 2015, Global Payments bought Realex Payments, an Irish-based payments gateway services company, for €115 million.[[11]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-11) On April 25, 2016, Global Payments completed the acquisition of [Heartland Payment Systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heartland_Payment_Systems \"Heartland Payment Systems\")[[7]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-Filkins2018.10.15-7) for $4.3 billion.[[12]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-12) Both Global Payments and its subsidiary Heartland Payment Systems were among the leading credit card processing companies in 2016, according to _[Business Insider](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business\\_Insider \"Business Insider\")_.[[13]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-13) Its headquarters moved from [Sandy Springs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy_Springs,_Georgia \"Sandy Springs, Georgia\"), Georgia to [Atlanta](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlanta \"Atlanta\"), Georgia in 2016.[[14]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-14)\n\nIn 2017, Global Payments initiated acquiring divisions of Active Networks.[[15]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-15) In 2018, Global Payments completed the acquisition of [AdvancedMD](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AdvancedMD \"AdvancedMD\").[[16]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-16) Also in 2018, Global Payments completed the acquisition of Sentral Education.[[17]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-17)\n\nOn May 28, 2019, Global Payments announced a $21.5 billion merger with [TSYS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TSYS \"TSYS\"). The merger was expected to trigger a [Federal Trade Commission](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Trade_Commission \"Federal Trade Commission\") investigation.[[18]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-18) However, no such investigation occurred.\n\nIn August 2022, Global Payments entered a definitive agreement to acquire EVO Payments Inc. for nearly $4 billion.[[19]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-19)[[20]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-20)\n\nOn May 1, 2023, the company announced that [CEO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_executive_officer \"Chief executive officer\") Jeffrey M. Sloan would resign, effective June 1, 2023. [COO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_operating_officer \"Chief operating officer\") Cameron M. Bready succeeded him as CEO and president.[[21]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-21) As of July 2023, no single COO of the company had been named. Instead, two individuals were appointed to oversee domestic and international operations as [Senior Executive Vice Presidents](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corporate_title \"Corporate title\"): Vince Lombardo and Bob Cortopassi, respectively.[[22]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-22)\n\nStarting in early 2024 and continuing into September, Global Payments began laying off staff. These layoffs took place in the US, Canada, UK and India, as well as other smaller regional markets. Approximately 5400 employees were laid off during this time.[[23]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-23) Just weeks before the September lay offs were announced, Global Payments reported its second quarter results. Global Payments reported a net income of $296.4 million, a significant increase of $46.6 million compared to $249.7 million in the same quarter of 2023.[[24]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-24)\n\nIn April 2025, Global Payments agreed to acquire [Worldpay](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worldpay,_Inc. \"Worldpay, Inc.\"), a payments technology provider, for $22 billion from [GTCR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GTCR \"GTCR\") and [FIS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIS_(company) \"FIS (company)\"). As part of this deal, it also agreed to sell its Issuer Solutions business, TSYS, to FIS for $13.5 billion.[[25]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-25)[[26]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-26) In January 2026, following regulatory approvals in multiple jurisdictions, it was announced the acquisition had been completed with a final transaction valued at $24.25 billion.[[27]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-27)\n\n## TSYS Acquisition\n\n[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Global_Payments&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: TSYS Acquisition\")]\n\nOn September 18, 2019, [TSYS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TSYS \"TSYS\") and Global Payments completed their merger agreement for $21.5 billion.[[28]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-28)\n\nIn January 2026, Global Payments sold TSYS to [FIS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FIS_(company) \"FIS (company)\") for $13.5 billion.[[29]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-29)\n\n## Services and operations\n\n[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Global_Payments&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Services and operations\")]\n\nGlobal Payments provides payment services directly to merchants and indirectly through other financial organizations. Its technology-enabled services also support integrated payments, [e-commerce](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E-commerce \"E-commerce\"), and [omni-channel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omnichannel#Retail \"Omnichannel\") services.[[4]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-tsysmerger-4)[[5]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-glimmers-5)\n\nGlobal Payments operates in more than 100 countries and serves 3.5 million merchants as well as 1,300 financial institutions.[[30]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-30) The company processes more than 50 billion transactions per year.[[31]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-31) After merging with TSYS in 2019, Global Payments has nearly 24,000 employees.[[32]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-32)\n\nIn 2020, 63 percent of the company's revenue was derived from \"Merchant Solutions\"[[33]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-33) 26 percent from Issuer Solutions,[[34]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-34) and 11 percent from the Business and Consumer Solutions segment,[[35]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-35) which operates as NetSpend.[[36]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-36) Eighty percent of Global Payments Merchant Solutions revenue was from North America, 15 percent was from Europe, and five percent was from Asia.[[37]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-37)\n\nAs of 2023, Global Payments does not have a singular [Chief Operating Officer (COO)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_operating_officer \"Chief operating officer\"). Instead the company divides operations into two distinct branches: domestic \"US Merchant Solutions\" headed by Senior Executive Vice President Vince Lombardo and international and vertical markets headed by Senior Executive Vice President Bob Cortopassi.[[38]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-38) Current CEO Cameron M. Bready was formerly the COO of the company, but no COO was announced as part of the succession plan.[[39]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-39)\n\n## Data breaches and faults\n\n[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Global_Payments&action=edit&section=4 \"Edit section: Data breaches and faults\")]\n\nIn 2012, a data breach at Global Payments affected 1.5 million credit and debit card numbers.[[40]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-40)[[41]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-41) This breach eventually cost the company around $100 million.[[42]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-42) In 2015, a technical fault with Global Payments' systems led to thousands of British businesses being unable to accept credit card payments on [Valentine's Day](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentine's_Day \"Valentine's Day\") weekend.[[43]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-43)\n\n## Lawsuits\n\n[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Global_Payments&action=edit&section=5 \"Edit section: Lawsuits\")]\n\n### _Story v. Heartland Payments Systems_\n\n[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Global_Payments&action=edit&section=6 \"Edit section: Story v. Heartland Payments Systems\")]\n\nIn 2019, Global Payments subsidiary Heartland Payments Systems was sued by Florida resident Max Story, alleging that Heartland defrauded parents of school children by leading them to believe that fees collected by the [MySchoolBucks](https://www.myschoolbucks.com/) online lunch payment service, which Heartland owns and operates and which is used by 2 million people with children in 30,000 schools in the US, were going to their children's schools when in fact the money was going to Heartland.[[44]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-:1-44) Shortly after the lawsuit was filed, Heartland deposited $40,000 into Story's bank account in an attempt to nullify Story's legal standing to sue, but Story refused to accept the payment and reversed the deposit.[[44]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-:1-44) To limit the number of people who could potentially join the lawsuit if it is granted [class action](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Class_action \"Class action\") status, Heartland also updated its [terms of service](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terms_of_service \"Terms of service\") in 2019 to require MySchoolBucks users to accept that they \"will not be permitted to participate in the Story case as a class member\".[[44]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_note-:1-44)\n\n## References\n\n[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Global_Payments&action=edit&section=7 \"Edit section: References\")]\n\n1.   [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_ref-10-K_2024_1-0 \"Jump up\")[\"US SEC: Form 10-K Global Payments Inc\"](https://www.sec.gov/ix?doc=/Archives/edgar/data/1123360/000112336025000011/gpn-20241231.htm). [U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Securities_and_Exchange_Commission \"U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission\"). February 14, 2025. Retrieved February 18, 2025.\n2.   [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_ref-2 \"Jump up\")[\"Global Payments and TSYS to combine in 'merger of equals'\"](https://www.bizjournals.com/atlanta/news/2019/05/28/global-payments-and-tsys-to-combine-in-merger-of.html?ana=e_n). [Atlanta Business Chronicle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_City_Business_Journals \"American City Business Journals\"). May 28, 2019. Retrieved August 10, 2021. Combined, Global Payments and TSYS will provide payments and software services to approximately 3.5 million predominantly small to mid-sized (SMB) merchant locations and more than 1,300 financial institutions across more than 100 countries.\n3.   [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_ref-3 \"Jump up\")[\"Fortune 500: Global Payments\"](https://fortune.com/company/global-payments/fortune500/). [Fortune](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortune_(magazine) \"Fortune (magazine)\"). June 2, 2021. Retrieved August 11, 2021. The company provides payment technology and software services; in all, it facilitates transactions for more than 3 million businesses. Global Payments makes its debut on the Fortune 500 this year, buoyed by a stellar 2020 in which its revenue rose 51%, to $7.4 billion, as the pandemic encouraged more consumers to adopt cashless payment.\n4.   [Jump up to: 1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_ref-tsysmerger_4-0)[2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Payments#cite_ref-tsysmerger_4-1)[\"Major Fintech Merger Speaks to Supremacy of Software in Age of Stripe, Square\"](https://web.archive.org/web/20210731132215/https://ch